{
  "summary": "Pauline Brunet, who leads Cursor's global forward deployed engineering (FDE) team, lays out an operating playbook for building an FDE function from scratch. Her core framework is a 2x2 matrix of customer digital maturity against product customizability: FDE only earns its cost in the middle band, and the corners are better served by self-service docs, traditional SaaS deployment, or advisory work. She is emphatic that FDE is not staff augmentation and not professional services — requests framed as 'we're understaffed' are a red flag, and a scope of 'take two FDEs for six months' is a recipe for failure. The rest of the talk is concrete operational advice: hire 5+ year engineers with customer-facing chops, define success and baselines up front, keep scope directional rather than fixed, always co-develop with a named customer team, and reduce every ROI story to increasing revenue, decreasing costs, or mitigating risk. Worth watching if you are standing up or scoping a deployed-engineering org; less so if you want technical detail on Cursor's products.",
  "key_points": [
    "Before building an FDE function, map your target customers' digital maturity against how customizable your product is; FDE fits a middle band, while the mature/low-customization and low-maturity/low-customization corners are better served by self-service documentation or a traditional SaaS deployment.",
    "FDE is explicitly not staff augmentation — when a customer says they need help because they are understaffed, Brunet treats it as a red flag and screens for it by asking who on the customer side will form the working team.",
    "Cursor's FDE projects are scoped with the economic buyer or a senior champion so the work maps to a strategic objective with real ROI, ensuring the deployed agents and automations are not turned off after the team leaves.",
    "Cursor hires 'unicorns' for the founding team — engineers with 5+ years of experience and customer-facing ability, no early-career hires yet — with a plan to split the role into more-technical and more-customer-facing tracks as the team grows.",
    "The team is organized by geography today but expects to move to industry alignment, because failing to speak an industry's lingo (payments, asset management, risk for a bank) immediately costs credibility.",
    "Scope should be directional rather than a fixed deliverable list, because the FDE team hasn't seen the customer's data, systems, or processes up front and both sides will want to pivot as they learn.",
    "Every engagement should define success with a measurable baseline at the start (e.g. a three-hour process reduced to 20 minutes) and then actually measure against it at the end.",
    "All ROI reduces to three things — increasing revenue, decreasing costs, or mitigating risk — and FDEs should over-communicate which one they're driving, since customers often fail to frame agent spend against the cost it displaces.",
    "The FDE team functions as the tip of the spear for edge-case use cases beyond the software development lifecycle (HR, finance, supply chain, e-commerce) and as the tightest product feedback loop the company has.",
    "Partner with system integrators and consultancies to cover work the FDE team shouldn't do — change management, broad rollouts of mature products — and to extend reach at scale."
  ],
  "notable_quotes": [
    {
      "text": "I have some pretty strong opinions about where it fits and where it's not a great use of your 10X engineers to invest that time in for your customers.",
      "timestamp_sec": 121,
      "why": "Frames the whole talk as an argument about where FDE does and does not belong."
    },
    {
      "text": "if you have a customer who is really mature in their digital transformation, they have engineers, um it's a low customization on your project, I would really just say provide your product in a self-service fashion, create give great documentation, probably not a great use of your FDE motion",
      "timestamp_sec": 156,
      "why": "States the matrix's most counterintuitive cell — don't deploy FDEs at your most capable customers."
    },
    {
      "text": "you don't want to be like a solution architect, you don't want to be writing down bugs, you don't want to be, you know, updating documentation.",
      "timestamp_sec": 234,
      "why": "Sharp negative definition of the role."
    },
    {
      "text": "I would be very mindful that you're not doing a product 101 201 session, that you're not like holding workshops for everyone in the company to be trained on your latest SaaS software. I think that's not a good use of the FTE function personally.",
      "timestamp_sec": 273,
      "why": "Names a specific failure mode teams drift into."
    },
    {
      "text": "Otherwise, they're going to get bored, rightfully so, and they're going to leave because you sold them FTE that wasn't FTE in my opinion.",
      "timestamp_sec": 310,
      "why": "Ties scoping discipline directly to retention of scarce talent."
    },
    {
      "text": "I always say, if you put in the latest and greatest tech in your organization, and you don't accompany people, no one's going to use it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 359,
      "why": "The change-management thesis underlying the role."
    },
    {
      "text": "the FTE team is so close to the customers, embedded in their organizations, they're going to be the first ones have a really good pulse on what we should build next as a company.",
      "timestamp_sec": 392,
      "why": "Argues FDE's second product is roadmap intelligence."
    },
    {
      "text": "when we walk away, it is a strict ROI for them. That means they're not going to turn things off when we leave, right?",
      "timestamp_sec": 500,
      "why": "A concrete, checkable definition of a successful engagement."
    },
    {
      "text": "the FTE team to me is the tip of the spear that is going to test out these new use cases with customers.",
      "timestamp_sec": 537,
      "why": "Positions FDE as product exploration, not delivery."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, if anyone says, uh \"Yeah, you have to do this. Uh we're understaffed.\" Red flag for me personally.",
      "timestamp_sec": 575,
      "why": "The single sharpest disqualification heuristic in the talk."
    },
    {
      "text": "my trick is I just ask for who are the people we're going to work with.",
      "timestamp_sec": 575,
      "why": "A one-question operational test for staff-aug disguised as partnership."
    },
    {
      "text": "we hire 5 plus years software engineers. We don't hire out of school. We don't hire early career professionals at this moment.",
      "timestamp_sec": 679,
      "why": "Specific, checkable hiring bar for a founding FDE team."
    },
    {
      "text": "when you talk to an industry and you don't use their lingo, you immediately lose credibility.",
      "timestamp_sec": 718,
      "why": "Justifies the planned shift from geography to industry alignment."
    },
    {
      "text": "One thing we always joke about on my team is what we're doing today is not what we're going to do 6 months from now.",
      "timestamp_sec": 786,
      "why": "Captures the deliberately impermanent org design."
    },
    {
      "text": "Try out a project with a customer. You might fail. That's okay. Right? Just actually try it out. You will learn so much more than if you're just kind of waiting and planning.",
      "timestamp_sec": 824,
      "why": "States a bias-to-action position others might dispute."
    },
    {
      "text": "you build credibility by being very honest about where our applications and our products and our platform are the right tools and where they're not.",
      "timestamp_sec": 889,
      "why": "Argues declining use cases is a commercial asset, not a loss."
    },
    {
      "text": "I think that you cannot just do like, \"Hey, take two FTEs for 6 months, do whatever you want with them.\" I think that's a recipe for failure.",
      "timestamp_sec": 995,
      "why": "Direct attack on the most common FDE contracting model."
    },
    {
      "text": "keep the scope directional. We're going to do phase one and two. We're going to automate these things. We're going to set up these agents for you. It's going to take 6 weeks. We're going to do as much or as little as we can.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1035,
      "why": "Concrete alternative to fixed-scope contracting."
    },
    {
      "text": "If you're doing it alone in their office in a little cubicle, we have a problem.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1101,
      "why": "Memorable test for whether co-development is actually happening."
    },
    {
      "text": "It's always three things, super simple. Am I increasing revenue? Am I decreasing costs? Or am I mitigating risks? That's it. Every company, as complex as they are, that's what they care about.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1172,
      "why": "The talk's compressed ROI framework."
    }
  ],
